=== SURVEY ===

# 1) Baseline route + page (must remain canonical when AI is off)

Backend: GET /api/v1/notifications/logs (NotificationHandler.GetLogs in
internal/api/notification_handler.go) returns a paginated list of
NotificationLog rows for the requested vehicle / severity / rule_id /
search / from / to / read state filter combination. The route is
unguarded (no AI gate) and is the deterministic source of truth for
inbox content.

Frontend: /notifications/inbox renders InboxPage which delegates to
InboxBody (web/src/features/notifications/components/InboxBody.tsx).
InboxBody owns:
  - URL-backed filter + view state (severity, vehicle_id, rule_id,
    search, read state, from/to, view mode)
  - Bulk selection + bulk actions (mark read, archive/restore, delete)
  - Auto-mark-read on open (opt-out via localStorage)
  - Per-row context menu
  - Day-grouped flat list AND threaded grouped list

The slice prompt's frontend route metadata (`/alerts/inbox`) is a
canonical-name placeholder; the actual SPA mount is /notifications/inbox.
Slice 0034 has the same divergence (registers `/alerts/studio` while the
page mounts at /notifications/studio); we keep the convention so the
9999 final-gate's frontend-route audit script — which only verifies
shape, not literal mount paths — keeps working.

# 2) Existing AI-feature wiring patterns (template = 0034
#    alert-tuning-suggestions, the immediately-preceding A-tier slice)

Strategy:
  internal/ai/strategies/alert-tuning-suggestions/strategy.go declares
  FeatureID="alert-tuning-suggestions", a system prompt that is the
  contract for the LLM, allowedTools=[draft_alert_rule_patch,
  validate_alert_rule], PolicyAlertBuilder redaction.

Tools:
  internal/ai/tools/alert_tuning.go — draft_alert_rule_patch is a typed
  read-only tool that proposes an AlertRule patch grounded in recent
  firing history. validate_alert_rule was already registered by N1.

Handler:
  internal/api/ai_alert_tuning_handler.go exposes ServeHTTP at
  POST /api/v1/ai/alerts/rules/{ruleID}/tune/draft, decodes a typed body,
  resolves the provider via *provider.Registry.For("alert-tuning-suggestions"),
  opens an SSE writer to the response, and runs
  dispatch.Dispatcher.Run(ctx, strategy, input, sseWriter).

Router wiring:
  internal/api/router.go imports the strategy, calls
  tools.RegisterAlertTuningSuggestionsTools(...) on the process tool
  registry, constructs the handler, and threads it as a parameter into
  mountAIRoutes(...). internal/api/ai_routes.go declares a stub-fallback
  inside the /ai/* sub-router so off-mode 404 holds even with a nil
  handler at boot.

Job stub:
  internal/jobs/ai_idle_drain_indexer.go is the canonical pattern for
  features that declare a JobNames entry: a fail-closed cron stub that
  re-checks ai_mode + per-feature toggle on every tick and returns
  {Skipped: 1} if either is off. The actual fan-out implementation
  lands in a future slice; the gate ships today.

SPA component:
  web/src/components/ai/AIAlertTuningSuggestions.tsx — wrapped with
  withAiFeature('alert-tuning-suggestions', InnerSection). Uses
  useAiStream targeting /ai/alerts/rules/{ruleID}/tune/draft. Captures
  tool_result for draft_alert_rule_patch into proposal state. "Apply
  to form" button copies the proposal into the parent's form state via
  onApplyDraft callback. Computed disabled, dedicated cleanup effect.

i18n:
  en.json carries settings.ai.features.alert-tuning-suggestions
  (label + description) plus notifications.alertStudio.aiTuning.* for
  the visible component copy. ar.json + he.json are placeholders that
  fall back to en via i18next's fallbackLng.

Eval goldens + canned:
  internal/ai/strategies/alert-tuning-suggestions/goldens.yaml has 3
  goldens (typical_noisy_rule, insufficient_history, refusal_other_rule)
  with paired canned/*.yaml provider replies for the F6 mock provider.

# 3) Inbox-categorization-specific shape

Source port shape:
  LoadCategoryCounts(ctx, filters database.NotificationLogFilters)
    -> ([]CategoryCount, totalInWindow int, minRequiredEvents int, error)
  The production adapter in internal/api/ai_inbox_categorization_handler.go
  composes NotificationRepo.GetLogsFiltered + per-unique-AlertID lookup
  via AlertRuleRepo.GetByID + tools.BucketByCategory.

Closed taxonomy (alphabetical, "other" last):
  ["battery", "charging", "climate", "connectivity", "maintenance",
   "noise", "security", "tire", "other"]

Substring-mapper ordering:
  CategoryForSignal in internal/ai/tools/inbox_auto_categorization.go
  maps signal_name to category by deterministic substring matching.
  Edge case: "tpms_warn" matches "tpms" (tire family) BEFORE "warn"
  (maintenance family) — verified by test
  TestCategoryForSignal/tpms_warn.

Tool registration:
  This slice registers BOTH new tools (draft_alert_categories +
  validate_alert_category) via tools.RegisterInboxAutoCategorizationTools.
  Unlike slice 0034 which reused validate_alert_rule from N1, slice
  0035's validate_alert_category is brand-new — categories live in
  this slice's closed taxonomy.

JobNames + PushKinds:
  - JobNames=["ai_alert_inbox_categorizer"]: declared in registry,
    implemented as a fail-closed gate stub at
    internal/jobs/ai_alert_inbox_categorizer.go. Tests assert the
    off-mode + toggle-off + settings-error fail-closed contract.
  - PushKinds=["ai_alert_category_suggested"]: declared only. The
    actual push fan-out worker is documented as future per-precedent
    slices (digest, yir).

=== REASONING ===

# Why preserve baseline by HOC composition, not handler branch

The propose-only contract (ADR-015 §I3 + §I8) requires that the
deterministic NotificationFilterBar + GET /api/v1/notifications/logs
path remains the SOLE write path for inbox state. We compose by:

  1. Wrapping the AI section with withAiFeature('inbox-auto-categorization',
     InnerSection) so the entire tree is ABSENT (returns null) when
     ai_mode='off' or the per-feature toggle is off. This is structural
     hiding, not CSS hiding — the DOM never carries a disabled AI
     button (ADR-015 §I5).
  2. Mounting /api/v1/ai/alerts/inbox/categorize ONLY through
     guard.Wrap("inbox-auto-categorization", ...). The guard returns
     404 BEFORE the handler runs in off mode, so even a misbehaving
     client cannot tickle the LLM (ADR-015 §I6 + §I7).
  3. Routing the "Apply" affordance back through the existing URL-backed
     rule_id filter, NOT a new write path. The AI panel's
     onApplyCategories callback only calls setFiltersBatch — the
     deterministic baseline is what writes to the URL and re-fetches
     /api/v1/notifications/logs. No new persistence path exists.

# Why register both tools in this slice instead of reusing existing ones

draft_alert_rule_patch (slice 0034) is shaped around tuning a SINGLE
AlertRule's thresholds; it is not reusable for multi-rule histogram
bucketing. validate_alert_rule (slice N1) validates a RULE PATCH, not
a CATEGORY LABEL. The closed category taxonomy and the histogram-shape
output are unique to this slice, so two new tools is the correct
factoring. They live in a single file
(internal/ai/tools/inbox_auto_categorization.go) so the closed-taxonomy
constants + substring mapper + bucketer are colocated and reusable for
the future ai_alert_inbox_categorizer fan-out worker.

# Why the closed-taxonomy substring mapper is deterministic

CategoryForSignal is pure: same input → same output, no external state,
no time dependence, no DB. The bucket counts the LLM sees come from
the same deterministic mapping the future fan-out worker will use, so
the LLM's narration stays consistent with the user's eventual filter
state. This is the classical LLM-grounding pattern: numbers are
computed by deterministic Go code; the LLM's job is purely descriptive
narration over those numbers.

# Why ai_alert_inbox_categorizer is a no-op gate today

The per-user category-recompute + push fan-out is naturally a
fan-out-and-batch worker (see methodology 0030 "Why a job and not an
inline …" reasoning). Shipping the fail-closed gate today proves the
ADR-015 §I12 #3 invariant ("background dispatcher gate trips before
execution") for the JobNames entry registered by this slice. The
actual fan-out implementation lands in a future slice when the push
worker arrives; today's gate ensures off-mode users never receive an
ai_alert_category_suggested push.

# Why the eval golden's substring pins are narrow

The first eval pass blocked on two false positives:
  - "forecast" appearing inside the canned reply's "NOT a forecast"
    disclaimer (substring match doesn't honor negation).
  - "batt" matching the legitimate "battery" canonical category label
    (the closed-taxonomy enforcement happens via validate_alert_category,
    not via answer-substring blocking).
Both pins were rephrased to target only the wrong-frame patterns
("predicts that", "will likely", "regression model") and only the
clearly out-of-taxonomy combined names ("battery_charging",
"batt/charge"), without false-positiving on the canonical labels or
the honest-method disclaimer.

